<!-- Issue descriptions below are snapshots of the linked GitHub issues. -->

# Introduction # {#introduction}

The HTML <{model}> element allows a website to embed interactive 3D
models as conveniently as any other visual media. Models are served as
a standalone resource. The <{model}> element also has support for
interaction and animation playback while presented within the page,
and supports more spatial experiences, such as the stereoscopic
display of 3D content where available.

As <{model}> is rendered directly by the user agent, it has
the ability to utilize privileged information, such as the user's
head and eye position or the lighting of the user's environment,
without exposing that information to JavaScript. Additionally, the use
of accessibility information and controls can be engaged in a
privacy-preserving manner.

As <{model}> is [=embedded content=], the user agent can present
3D content in a privacy-preserving, interactive, and accessible manner.

# Out of scope # {#out-of-scope}

There are a number of specified non-goals for this initial incubation;
some are beyond the long-term scope of this proposal, and others are
deferred for the benefit of reaching consensus on an initial
specification, which can hopefully serve the needs of users and authors
sooner.

## Scene graph inspection and manipulation ## {#scene-graph-inspection-and-manipulation}

While the use cases for inspecting and manipulating the contents of a
given model asset are clear, the initial scope of this proposal is
intentionally limited to a reduced complexity, hopefully making the
initial specification one that has broad consensus amongst the web
community.

## Animation mixing and blending ## {#animation-mixing-and-blending}

While many popular model formats include the ability to encode, play
and mix multiple animations in an animation mixing system, This initial
specification considers only a single animation timeline, both for
simplicity and for having a minimal viable specification on which the
web community can build on.

## Stateful interaction with <{model}> contents ## {#stateful-interaction-with-model-contents}

While many popular model formats support tapping on a 3D button inside
their contents to show or hide additional contents like a title, play
an animation or undertake some other stateful action. Because there is
no current intention to provide a JavaScript-facing mechanism to be
aware of such actions, stateful interactions should be considered out
of scope.

## Subresource loading ## {#subresource-loading}

While many popular model formats support the use of external asset
requests for better re-use and consistency, this initial specification
will only allow assets that are self-contained.

# Examples # {#examples}

## Adding a model to a document ## {#adding-a-model-to-a-document}

<div class="example" id="example-simple-model">

The following example shows the simplest way to add a 3D model to a
document:

```html
<model src="3d-assets/car"></model>
```

By using content negotiation the user agent relies on the server to
send back a 3D model in a format the user agent supports.

</div>

## Enabling orbit stage mode ## {#enabling-orbit-stage-mode}

<div class="example" id="example-orbit-stage-mode">

The following example shows the syntax for enabling the standard "orbit" mode:

```html
<model stagemode="orbit">
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.usdz" type="model/vnd.pixar.usd">
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.glb" type="model/gltf-binary">
</model>
```

</div>

## Supporting multiple formats ## {#supporting-multiple-formats}

<div class="example" id="example-multiple-formats">

The following example shows a list of potential source formats
that can be selected from:

```html
<model>
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.usdz" type="model/vnd.pixar.usd">
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.glb" type="model/gltf-binary">
</model>
```

The [=select a model source|model source selection algorithm=] gives a valid,
non-empty <{model/src}> URL precedence over <{source}> children. Otherwise,
it selects the first child with a valid URL, a supported type if specified,
and a matching media query if specified.

</div>

## Providing fallback content for legacy user agents ## {#providing-fallback-content-for-legacy-user-agents}

<div class="example" id="example-fallback-content">

The following example supplies fallback content for user agents that
do not yet support the element:

```html
<model>
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.usdz" type="model/vnd.pixar.usd">
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.glb" type="model/gltf-binary">
  <img src="image-assets/teapot.png">
</model>
```

</div>

## Making <{model}> accessible ## {#making-model-accessible}

<div class="example" id="example-accessible-model">

The following example supplies alt text information for both
the model content and fallback content, in the event that model
is not supported on the target user agent.

```html
<model>
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.usdz" type="model/vnd.usdz+zip">
  <source src="3d-assets/teapot.glb" type="model/gltf-binary">
  <img src="image-assets/teapot.png" alt="A teapot">
</model>
```

</div>

## Providing a loading preview and fallback content ## {#example-model-poster}

<div class="example" id="example-poster-and-fallback">

The poster provides an image preview while the model loads. The fallback content
is displayed if the model cannot be loaded, loading is blocked, or the browser
does not support <{model}>. A <{picture}> in the fallback content can select images
for different color schemes and formats:

```html
<model src="3d-assets/teapot.usdz" poster="image-assets/teapot-preview.png">
  <picture>
    <source media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)"
            srcset="image-assets/teapot-dark.avif" type="image/avif">
    <source srcset="image-assets/teapot-light.avif" type="image/avif">
    <img src="image-assets/teapot.png" alt="A ceramic teapot">
  </picture>
</model>
```

</div>

# The <dfn element>model</dfn> element # {#the-model-element}

: Categories
:: [=Flow content=].
:: [=Phrasing content=].
:: [=Embedded content=].
:: If the element's <{model/stagemode}> attribute is in the `orbit` state:
    [=interactive content=].
:: [=Palpable content=].

: Contexts in which this element can be used:
:: Where [=embedded content=] is expected.

: Content model:
:: If the element has a <{model/src}> attribute: [=transparent|Transparent=].
:: If the element has no <{model/src}> attribute: Zero or more <{source}> children,
    then [=transparent=], optionally intermixed with [=script-supporting elements=].

: Tag omission in text/html:
:: Neither tag is omissible.

: Content Attributes:
:: [[HTML#global-attributes|Global attributes]]
:: <{model/autoplay}> — Start the model's animation automatically when the resource is loaded
:: <{model/stagemode}> — Allows the user to interact with the model in a specified mode
:: <{model/crossorigin}> — Credentials mode for model, environment-map, and poster requests
:: <{model/height}> — Vertical dimension
:: <{model/loop}> — Whether to repeat the model's animation
:: <{model/poster}> — Image preview to show before the model is ready to display
:: <{model/src}> — Address of the resource
:: <{model/width}> — Horizontal dimension

: DOM Interface
:: The {{HTMLModelElement}} interface provides a means to interface with
    the embedded resource.

<pre class="idl" id="htmlmodelelement-interface">
[Exposed=Window, SecureContext]
interface HTMLModelElement : HTMLElement {
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute unsigned long width;
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute unsigned long height;
  [CEReactions, ReflectURL] attribute USVString src;
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute boolean autoplay;
  [CEReactions, Reflect] attribute boolean loop;
  [CEReactions] attribute DOMString? crossOrigin;
  [CEReactions, ReflectURL] attribute USVString poster;

  readonly attribute Promise&lt;HTMLModelElement&gt; ready;
  readonly attribute DOMPointReadOnly boundingBoxCenter;
  readonly attribute DOMPointReadOnly boundingBoxExtents;

  attribute DOMMatrixReadOnly entityTransform;

  attribute USVString environmentMap;
  readonly attribute Promise&lt;undefined&gt; environmentMapReady;

  [Reflect=stagemode] attribute DOMString stageMode;
};
</pre>

The <{model}> element is used for embedding 3D models into a document.
Its model-specific APIs, resource processing, and rendering are restricted to
[=secure contexts=], as specified in [[#model-secure-contexts]].

Content may be provided inside the <{model}> element as [=fallback content=].
The element MUST represent this content when model loading is blocked or fails,
as specified in [[#model-poster-display]]. It is also intended for browsers that
do not support <{model}>. User agents MUST NOT show the fallback content while
the model is loading or while available model content is displayed.

## <dfn element-attr for="model">autoplay</dfn> attribute ## {#autoplay-attribute}

The <{model/autoplay}> attribute is a [=boolean attribute=] that controls whether
the model's animation starts automatically when a new model resource is loaded.
The {{HTMLModelElement/autoplay}} IDL attribute MUST [=reflect=] the content
attribute.

After the initial bounds calculation and fitting for a newly loaded model
resource, and before resolving {{HTMLModelElement/ready}}, the user agent MUST
[=initialize model animation playback=] for the element.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="initialize-model-animation-playback">

To <dfn noexport>initialize model animation playback</dfn> for a <{model}> element
|element|:

  1. If |element|'s loaded model resource contains no animation, return.
  1. Set the animation to its first frame and initialize playback in the paused
    state.
  1. If |element| has an <{model/autoplay}> attribute, begin animation playback.

</div>

After playback has been initialized, adding, removing, or changing
<{model/autoplay}> MUST NOT by itself start, pause, or restart the current
animation. Its value is used when initializing a subsequently loaded model
resource; recreating a renderer for the same resource MUST NOT reapply autoplay
in place of the resource's existing playback state.

Note: Autoplay controls initial animation playback and does not require eager
  resource loading. A user agent can still defer loading an offscreen model.

## <dfn element-attr for="model">stagemode</dfn> attribute ## {#stagemode-attribute}

The <{model/stagemode}> attribute determines the model's stage mode. It is an
[=enumerated attribute=]. The <dfn attr-value for="model/stagemode">none</dfn>
keyword selects the [[#stage-mode-none|none mode]], and the
<dfn attr-value for="model/stagemode">orbit</dfn> keyword selects the
[[#stage-mode-orbit|orbit mode]]. Its missing value default and invalid value
default are the `none` state.

Changes to the selected mode MUST apply the behavior defined in [[#interaction]]
and [[#automatic-fitting]]. The {{HTMLModelElement/stageMode}} IDL attribute
MUST [=reflect=] the <{model/stagemode}> content attribute.

## <dfn element-attr for="model">controls</dfn> attribute ## {#controls-attribute}

## <dfn element-attr for="model">crossorigin</dfn> attribute ## {#crossorigin-attribute}

The <{model/crossorigin}> attribute is a [=CORS settings attribute=]. It controls
request credentials using HTML's [=CORS settings attribute credentials mode=],
as specified in [[#cors]].

The {{HTMLModelElement/crossOrigin}} IDL attribute MUST [=reflect=] the
<{model/crossorigin}> content attribute, [=limited to only known values=].

## <dfn element-attr for="model">height</dfn> attribute ## {#height-attribute}

The <{model/height}> attribute, if specified, MUST have a value that is a
[=valid non-negative integer=]. It gives the desired vertical dimension
of the <{model}> element in CSS pixels. Zero is permitted.

## <dfn element-attr for="model">loop</dfn> attribute ## {#loop-attribute}

The <{model/loop}> attribute is a [=boolean attribute=] that controls repetition
of the model's animation. The {{HTMLModelElement/loop}} IDL attribute MUST
[=reflect=] the content attribute.

When a playing animation with non-zero duration reaches its end during forward
playback or its beginning during reverse playback, the user agent MUST
[=handle a model animation boundary=] for the element.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="handle-model-animation-boundary">

To <dfn noexport>handle a model animation boundary</dfn> for a <{model}> element
|element|:

  1. If |element| has no <{model/loop}> attribute, pause the animation at the
    endpoint it reached and return.
  1. If playback is forward, seek to the animation's beginning; otherwise, seek
    to its end.
  1. Continue playback with the same direction and speed.

</div>

Changes to <{model/loop}> MUST affect subsequent boundary handling. They MUST NOT
by themselves start, pause, or restart playback.

Note: The [HTML media loop attribute](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#attr-media-loop)
  describes the same forward-playback behavior. Model looping also applies to
  reverse playback.

## <dfn element-attr for="model">poster</dfn> attribute ## {#poster-attribute}

The <{model/poster}> attribute gives the URL of an image file that represents the
model and is displayed before the model is ready to display. The attribute, if
present, MUST contain a [=valid non-empty URL potentially surrounded by spaces=].

Each <{model}> element has a <dfn for="model" noexport id="model-poster-frame">poster frame</dfn>
that is an image or null, initially null.

When a <{model}> element is created, or its <{model/poster}> attribute is set,
changed, or removed, the user agent MUST [=determine a model poster frame=] for
the element, regardless of whether the poster is currently displayed. A change
to the document's base URL alone MUST NOT restart this algorithm.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="determine-model-poster-frame">

To <dfn noexport>determine a model poster frame</dfn> for a <{model}> element |element|:

  1. Abort any existing instance of this algorithm for |element| without changing
    its [=model/poster frame=]. Results from an aborted instance MUST NOT
    subsequently change the poster frame.
  1. If |element|'s <{model/poster}> attribute is absent or its value is the empty
    string, set |element|'s [=model/poster frame=] to null and return.
  1. Let |url| be the result of [=encoding-parsing a URL=] given the
    <{model/poster}> attribute's value, relative to |element|'s [=node document=].
  1. If |url| is failure, set |element|'s [=model/poster frame=] to null and return.
  1. Let |request| be a new [=/request=] whose [=request/URL=] is |url| and
    [=request/destination=] is `"image"`.
  1. [=Configure a model request=] given |request| and |element|.
  1. [=Fetch=] |request|. This MUST [=delays the load event|delay the load event=]
    of |element|'s [=node document=] until this instance of the algorithm
    completes or is aborted.
  1. When fetching and image decoding complete, if an image is obtained, set
    |element|'s [=model/poster frame=] to that image. Otherwise, set it to null.

</div>

Loading or changing a poster image MUST NOT interrupt available model content.
The poster's availability MUST NOT be a condition for resolving
{{HTMLModelElement/ready}}, and a failure to fetch or decode the poster MUST NOT
reject that promise or cause the element to display its fallback content.

The {{HTMLModelElement/poster}} IDL attribute MUST [=reflect=] the
<{model/poster}> content attribute as a URL.

Note: Poster URL parsing, cancellation, and load-event behavior follow the
  [poster-frame retrieval rules for `<video>`](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/media.html#attr-video-poster)
  in [[HTML]]. Request construction follows [[#cors]], and
  poster display follows [[#model-poster-display]].

ISSUE: <span id="issue-130"></span> Like <{video/poster}>, <{model/poster}> accepts a single image URL.
  Should model loading previews also support alternative formats, resolutions,
  aspect ratios, or color schemes, or richer placeholders such as another model?
  A <{picture}> in fallback content supports image selection after a model fails,
  but does not provide a responsive loading poster. See [GitHub issue #130:
  What role, if any, should poster play?](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/130).

## <dfn element-attr for="model">src</dfn> attribute ## {#src-attribute}

The <{model/src}> attribute gives the URL of the model resource. If present, it
MUST contain a [=valid non-empty URL potentially surrounded by spaces=]. The
{{HTMLModelElement/src}} IDL attribute MUST [=reflect=] the content attribute as a
URL.

## <dfn element-attr for="model">width</dfn> attribute ## {#width-attribute}

The <{model/width}> attribute, if specified, MUST have a value that is a
[=valid non-negative integer=]. It gives the desired horizontal dimension
of the <{model}> element in CSS pixels. Zero is permitted.

# <{source}> element integration # {#source-element-integration}

## The <{source}> element's parent is a <{model}> element ## {#source-parent-model}

The <{source}> element is permitted as a child of <{model}>, in the position
specified by the <{model}> element's content model. Its <{source/src}> attribute
MUST be present and contain a [=valid non-empty URL potentially surrounded by spaces=]
identifying a model resource. The optional <{source/type}> attribute MUST contain
a [=valid MIME type string=] and indicates the model format, allowing unsupported
formats to be skipped before fetching. The optional <{source/media}> attribute
MUST contain a [=valid media query list=] and is evaluated using HTML's
[=matches the environment=] definition, with the media type fixed to
''@media/screen'' and media features taken from the parent <{model}>'s
[=node document=].

The <{source/srcset}>, <{source/sizes}>, <{source/width}>, and <{source/height}>
attributes MUST NOT be specified when the parent is <{model}>. They have no effect
on model source selection or the model element's dimensions.

Each <{model}> element has a <dfn for="model" noexport>selected source URL</dfn>,
initially null.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="select-model-source">

To <dfn noexport>select a model source</dfn> for a <{model}> element |element|,
return a URL or null as follows:

  1. If |element|'s [=node document=]'s [=Document/browsing context=] is null,
    return null.
  1. Let |candidates| be a [=list=] containing |element|, followed by its
    <{source}> children in [=tree order=].
  1. For each |candidate| of |candidates|:
    1. If |candidate| is a <{source}> element with a non-empty <{source/type}>
        value indicating a model format the user agent does not support, continue.
    1. If |candidate| is a <{source}> element with a <{source/media}> attribute
        whose value does not [=matches the environment|match the environment=],
        using the ''@media/screen'' media type, continue.
    1. Let |value| be |candidate|'s `src` attribute value, or the empty string
        if the attribute is absent.
    1. Set |value| to the result of
        [=strip leading and trailing ASCII whitespace|stripping leading and trailing ASCII whitespace=]
        from |value|.
    1. If |value| is the empty string, continue.
    1. Let |url| be the result of [=encoding-parsing a URL=] given |value|,
        relative to |candidate|'s [=node document=].
    1. If |url| is not failure, return |url|.
  1. Return null.

</div>

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="update-model-source">

To <dfn noexport>update the model source</dfn> for a <{model}> element |element|:

  1. Let |url| be the result of [=select a model source=] for |element|.
  1. If |url| and |element|'s [=model/selected source URL=] are both null, return.
  1. If both are URLs whose [=URL serializer|serializations=] are identical, return.
  1. Abort any pending model fetch and decoding operations for |element| and
    discard its previous model content, stored bounds, and animation playback state.
    Any completion of an aborted load MUST be ignored.
  1. [=Update the model entity transform=] for |element|, given the identity matrix.
  1. Set |element|'s {{HTMLModelElement/boundingBoxCenter}} and
    {{HTMLModelElement/boundingBoxExtents}} to separate new {{DOMPointReadOnly}}
    objects with X, Y, and Z components set to zero and W set to one.
  1. Reject |element|'s unsettled {{HTMLModelElement/ready}} promise, if any,
    with an {{AbortError}} exception, and replace it with [=a new promise=].
  1. Set |element|'s [=model/selected source URL=] to |url|.
  1. If |url| is null, [=queue an element task=] on the
    [=DOM manipulation task source=] given |element| to [=fire an event=]
    named `error` at |element|.
  1. Otherwise, begin loading that resource, subject to
    [[#model-secure-contexts]], [[#cors]], and [[#content-security-policy]].
    The user agent MAY defer fetching as for other model loads.
  1. Re-evaluate [[#model-poster-display]].

</div>

Failure to fetch or decode the selected resource MUST follow
[[#model-fetch-failures]] and [[#model-poster-display]]. It MUST NOT, by itself,
cause selection of a later <{source}> element.

Note: Unlike <{picture}>, media queries are evaluated when model source selection
runs. Changes in the environment alone do not trigger source selection. Unlike
<{audio}> and <{video}>, changing an existing <{source}>'s attributes can change
the selected model resource.

When a <{model}> element is created, or its <{model/src}> attribute is set,
changed, or removed, the user agent MUST [=update the model source=] for it.

As additional [=attribute change steps=] for <{source}>, setting, changing,
or removing its <{source/src}>, <{source/type}>, or <{source/media}> attribute
MUST [=update the model source=] for its parent if the parent is <{model}>.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="model-source-insertion-steps">

The <{source}> [=source insertion steps|HTML element insertion steps=],
given |insertedNode|, are extended with the following additional step:

  1. If |insertedNode|'s parent is a <{model}> element,
    [=update the model source=] for that parent.

</div>

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="model-source-removing-steps">

The <{source}> [=source removing steps|HTML element removing steps=],
given <var ignore>removedNode</var>, |isSubtreeRoot|, and |oldAncestor|,
are extended with the following additional step:

  1. If |isSubtreeRoot| is true and |oldAncestor| is a <{model}> element,
    [=update the model source=] for |oldAncestor|.

</div>

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="model-source-moving-steps">

The <{source}> [=source moving steps|HTML element moving steps=],
given |movedNode|, |isSubtreeRoot|, and |oldAncestor|,
are extended with the following additional steps:

  1. If |isSubtreeRoot| is false, return.
  1. If |oldAncestor| is a <{model}> element,
    [=update the model source=] for |oldAncestor|.
  1. If |movedNode|'s parent is a <{model}> element other than |oldAncestor|,
    [=update the model source=] for that parent.

</div>

# Interaction # {#interaction}

In addition to emitting standard input events, model may interpret input events according to
specific stage modes, including `none` and `orbit`.

## none ## {#stage-mode-none}

The default stage mode. In this mode, input events do not have
any direct action on the behavior of the element.

## orbit ## {#stage-mode-orbit}

In this mode, input events in a horizontal direction result in a rotation of the model about
the Y axis, and events in a vertical direction result in a rotation about the horizontal axis.
This is reflected in the model's {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} value.

In this mode, the {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} value is read-only.

Setting the mode to orbit results in a change of the position and scale to the
<dfn noexport>orbit fit</dfn> mode.

ISSUE: <span id="issue-2"></span> glTF is not a run-time format. It does not define what an application should do with a model
  once it is loaded and rendered. It does provide some capabilities that a run-time engine may use
  to enhance the user experience. glTF currently does not store any interactivity information.
  Currently that is solely a run-time determination. The run-time determines what parts (if any) of
  the model may be active and the behavior based on any trigger.
  Like Interactivity,
  animation is not built-into glTF. glTF files may contain animation parameters that specify the
  type of animation (e.g., morph, skin & bones, etc.) and the associated parameters needed to
  perform the animation. There is nothing in the glTF specification that defines how one animation
  interacts with another. For example, a human model may include walk, jump, and drop animations;
  but it is unlikely that they should all be played at the same time.
  Any HTML element that
  wishes to handle animation as stored in a glTF file needs to understand how the content creator
  intended the animation to play. [GitHub issue #2: Dealing with format specific animations and Interaction](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/2).

# Controls # {#controls}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-43"></span> As with other media elements (again [#13](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/13)), having
  "controls" for media specific things can be extremely helpful for accessibility (and
  just generally helpful for developers not needing to deal with things like the fullscreen
  API).
  It would be nice to consider adding support for `controls` and then
  leaving it mostly to the UA as to what those controls are... we could figure out a standard set of
  things, like <{video}> provides. [GitHub issue #43: Adding "controls"](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/43).

# Rendering # {#rendering}

The model SHOULD be rendered according to a realtime, physically-based rendering (PBR) shading
model, and lit by an image-based light.

## Environment map ## {#environment-map}

If provided, an environment map MUST be interpreted as an equirectangular environment map.
If an {{HTMLModelElement/environmentMap}} is not specified, the User Agent MUST provide
an appropriate map.

## Model pose ## {#model-pose}

The position, rotation, and scale of a displayed model MUST present its contents according
to its {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} property, a {{DOMMatrixReadOnly}} that can be
composed using that object's existing API.

The point and matrix interfaces and their operations are defined in [[GEOMETRY-1]].

Each <{model}> element has a <dfn noexport>stored entity transform</dfn>, initially
a {{DOMMatrixReadOnly}} representing the identity matrix with
{{DOMMatrixReadOnly/is2D}} set to false.

For the setter steps below, an element's <dfn noexport>model renderer</dfn> is the
user agent's internal instance that manages loading and rendering the element's
model, or retained rendering state used while rendering is suspended. It is null
until such an instance is created, and becomes null when that instance and its
retained state are discarded. A renderer can exist before
{{HTMLModelElement/ready}} resolves; its existence does not require the model to
be visible.

Updates to the {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} SHOULD be reflected on the next rendered frame.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="get-model-entity-transform">

The {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} getter steps are:

  1. Return this element's [=stored entity transform=].

</div>

### Restrictions on script-assigned transforms ### {#entity-transform-restrictions}

A matrix assigned to {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} by script MUST be
representable as a composition of scale, rotation, and translation, without
shear or perspective. Its scale factors MUST be non-zero and uniform across the
X, Y, and Z axes: the scale factors in that decomposition MUST have the same sign
and magnitude. All matrix components MUST be finite.

When validating a matrix, the user agent MAY account for floating-point rounding
in the scale factors and in reconstructing the matrix from its scale, rotation,
and translation. It MUST NOT replace an unsupported matrix with an approximation
that discards non-uniform scaling, shear, or perspective.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="set-model-entity-transform">

The {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} setter steps, given |value|, are:

  1. Let |element| be this <{model}> element.
  1. If |element| is in `orbit` mode, throw an {{InvalidStateError}} exception.
  1. If |element|'s [=model renderer=] is null, throw an {{UnknownError}} exception.
  1. Let |matrix| be a copy of |value|'s 16 matrix components.
  1. If |matrix| does not satisfy the matrix restrictions above, throw a
    {{NotSupportedError}} exception.
  1. [=Update the model entity transform=] for |element|, given |matrix|.
  1. Set |element|'s [=automatic fitting enabled=] flag to false.

</div>

If the setter throws, the [=stored entity transform=] and
[=automatic fitting enabled=] flag MUST remain unchanged. The model MUST continue
to use its previous transform.

These validation requirements apply to script assignments. They do not prevent
the user agent's fitting algorithms from producing a zero scale when an element
has a zero-sized viewport, as specified below.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="update-model-entity-transform">

To <dfn noexport>update the model entity transform</dfn> for a <{model}> element
|element|, given a matrix |matrix|:

  1. Let |transform| be a new {{DOMMatrixReadOnly}} initialized with the 16
    components of |matrix|, with {{DOMMatrixReadOnly/is2D}} set to false.
  1. Set |element|'s [=stored entity transform=] to |transform|.

</div>

This internal operation does not invoke the
{{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} setter, validate script-assigned transforms,
or change the [=automatic fitting enabled=] flag. Fitting and user-agent-controlled
orbit rotations MUST use this operation to expose their updated transforms. A
previously returned {{DOMMatrixReadOnly}} remains unchanged when a new transform
is stored.

### Coordinate spaces and length units ### {#model-coordinate-spaces}

The model APIs distinguish the following coordinate spaces:

: <dfn>model space</dfn>
:: The resource's coordinate space before {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} is applied,
    normalized to a right-handed, Y-up coordinate system. One unit of length in this space
    represents one meter of the asset's geometry. Normalization preserves the resource's
    origin; fitting does not modify this coordinate space.

: <dfn>element space</dfn>
:: The coordinate space of the model element's [=content box=],
    as defined in [[CSS-BOX-3]]. Its origin is the center of
    the viewport plane, with positive X pointing right, positive Y pointing up, and positive
    Z pointing toward the viewer. One unit of length in this space is one
    <dfn>CSS-derived meter</dfn>, equal to 100 CSS centimeters. The fixed ratios between
    [=absolute length|absolute length units=] in [[CSS-VALUES-4]],
    `1in = 96px = 2.54cm`, give the conversion
    `1 CSS px = 0.0254 / 96 CSS-derived meters`. This specification defines
    the term "CSS-derived meter"; CSS does not define an `m` unit.

: <dfn>physical presentation space</dfn>
:: The device-dependent space in which the user agent presents the page, including any
    spatial or stereoscopic presentation. Its mapping from [=element space=] may depend on
    zoom, display characteristics, and the user agent's presentation policy. The model APIs
    do not expose this mapping or guarantee a model's physical size on the device.

The user agent MUST convert the resource's geometry and internal transform translations
to [=model space=] using the length units and axis conventions declared by the resource
format, including any defaults specified by that format. If the format neither declares
nor specifies default length units, one resource length unit MUST be treated as one meter.

ISSUE: The one-meter fallback for formats that neither declare length units nor specify
  default length units needs further discussion. Is one meter an appropriate default,
  or should a different fallback be used? See [GitHub issue #155](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/155).

{{HTMLModelElement/boundingBoxCenter}} and {{HTMLModelElement/boundingBoxExtents}} MUST
report their X, Y, and Z components in meters in [=model space=]; the homogeneous W
component is dimensionless. These values MUST NOT include automatic
fitting, {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}}, CSS transforms on the element, or the
mapping to [=physical presentation space=].

{{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} MUST map [=model space=] to [=element space=]. Its
translation components are expressed in [=CSS-derived meters=], while scale factors and
rotation coefficients are dimensionless. This mapping precedes CSS transforms on the
element and the mapping to [=physical presentation space=].

Point transformation uses the [=transform a point with a matrix=]
algorithm in [[GEOMETRY-1]]. This specification defines the coordinate spaces and
length units used by the model APIs.

For fitting, the user agent MUST convert the content box's width and height from CSS
pixels to [=CSS-derived meters=] by multiplying each dimension by `0.0254 / 96`.
Device pixels MUST NOT be used for this calculation.

Browser zoom in this section refers to [=page zoom=] and visual
viewport scaling, as distinguished in the [[CSSOM-VIEW-1#zooming|Zooming section]] of [[CSSOM-VIEW-1]].
Page zoom affects the size of the initial viewport. Visual viewport scaling, exposed by
{{VisualViewport/scale}}, magnifies the page without affecting the initial viewport.

Page zoom and visual viewport scaling MUST NOT change the units defined above or the
model-space bounds of a resource. If page zoom changes the layout dimensions used by a
fitting algorithm, those dimensions MUST be converted using the same CSS-pixel conversion.
This does not change when the fitting algorithms run.

The {{Window/devicePixelRatio}} algorithm in [[CSSOM-VIEW-1]] uses the size of a CSS pixel
at the current page zoom, with a visual viewport scale factor of 1.0, divided by the size
of a device pixel. Page zoom can therefore change {{Window/devicePixelRatio}}; visual
viewport scaling is excluded from that calculation. Changes to
{{Window/devicePixelRatio}} or rasterization resolution MUST NOT change the API units,
model-space bounds, or the conversion from CSS pixels to [=element space=].

Note: <span id="issue-155"></span>These distinctions address the default length-unit question raised in [GitHub issue #155](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/155).
A meter of asset geometry and a CSS-derived meter of element space are not guarantees
of one physical meter in the device's presentation. The absolute-unit anchoring rules
and [=reference pixel=] in [[CSS-VALUES-4]] explain why CSS
physical units need not match physical measurements on a screen. Authors should not multiply
element-space translations by {{Window/devicePixelRatio}}.

<div class="example" id="model-unit-conversion-example">

A model-space bounding-box width of 2 represents a two-meter-wide asset, independent
of fitting or zoom. A 300 CSS-pixel-wide viewport is
`300 × 0.0254 / 96 = 0.079375` CSS-derived meters wide. If width limits the
fit, the uniform scale is `0.079375 / 2 = 0.0396875`.
To move an already fitted model one CSS pixel to the right in element space,
apply the translation after the existing model-to-element mapping:

```js
const cssPixelsToMeters = 0.0254 / 96;
const translation = new DOMMatrix().translate(cssPixelsToMeters, 0, 0);
model.entityTransform = translation.multiply(model.entityTransform);
```

Pre-multiplying by the translation makes it element-relative rather than scaling
it by the existing model fit. No device-pixel-ratio adjustment is needed.

</div>

### Model bounds and fitting ### {#model-bounds-and-fitting}

When a new model resource is loaded, the user agent MUST run the
[=bounding box calculation algorithm=] and store the resulting model-space bounds.
The [=stored entity transform=] MUST be set so that
the object is fully in view within the model element's laid-out content box
on the page. Initial fitting uses the [=model fit algorithm=] with an identity rotation
in `none` mode, or the [=orbit fit algorithm=] in `orbit` mode.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="bounding-box-calculation">

The <dfn>bounding box calculation algorithm</dfn> consists of the following steps.

  1. With the model scene loaded and normalized to [=model space=], set the animation to
    the first frame if present.
  1. let |max| be a [=new=] {{DOMPoint}} with values of `-Infinity`.
  1. let |min| be a [=new=] {{DOMPoint}} with values of `Infinity`.
  1. let |queue| be an empty [=list=] of elements.
  1. [=list/append|Add=] the model's root object to |queue|.
  1. While |queue| is not empty:
    1. set |element| to the last element in |queue| and [=list/remove|remove=] it from |queue|.
    1. If |element| contains any child elements, [=list/extend|add=] them to |queue|.
    1. If |element| contains renderable geometry, find the minimum and maximum value for
        the X, Y and Z locations of that geometry.
    1. Apply the world matrix of |element| to the bounding box of its geometry.
    1. set each value of |min| to the minimum of its current value and the minimum for
        this element's bounding box.
    1. set each value of |max| to the maximum of its current value and the maximum for
        this element's bounding box.
  1. Set the values of {{HTMLModelElement/boundingBoxCenter}} to be the mean of each values
    of |min| and |max|.
  1. Set the values of {{HTMLModelElement/boundingBoxExtents}} to be each value of |min|
    subtracted from |max|.

</div>

The fitting algorithms MUST use the width and height of the element's laid-out
[=content box=], converted to [=CSS-derived meters=] in [=element space=].
These dimensions result from CSS layout, using the
[=used value|used values=] defined in [[CSS-CASCADE-5]] and
the [=sizing properties=] defined in [[CSS-SIZING-3]]. They are not
the numerical values of the <{model/width}> and <{model/height}> attributes. CSS sizing
and layout constraints can cause them to differ from the desired dimensions given by
those attributes. The bounds remain in
[=model space=], before {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} is applied. Subsequent fitting
updates MUST use the stored bounds of the loaded resource; they MUST NOT repeat the
[=bounding box calculation algorithm=] or restart or seek the animation.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="model-fit">

The <dfn noexport>model fit algorithm</dfn> takes a |rotation| argument and consists of the
following steps.

  1. Let |bounds| be the resource's stored bounds, previously calculated using the
    [=bounding box calculation algorithm=].
  1. Let |extents| be the extents of |bounds|.
  1. Let |center| be the center of |bounds|.
  1. Let |width| and |height| be the laid-out content-box dimensions in CSS pixels, each
    multiplied by `0.0254 / 96` to obtain [=CSS-derived meters=] in [=element space=].
  1. Let |xScale| be |width| divided by |extents|.x, or positive infinity if |extents|.x is zero.
  1. Let |yScale| be |height| divided by |extents|.y, or positive infinity if |extents|.y is zero.
  1. Let |scale| be the minimum of |xScale| and |yScale|. If both are positive infinity, set
    |scale| to 1. If either viewport dimension is zero, set |scale| to 0.
  1. Let |transform| be a matrix with a uniform scale of |scale| and the supplied
    |rotation|.
  1. Set the translation in |transform| so that |center|, after applying the scale
    and rotation, is aligned with the center of the viewport in X and Y, and is
    |scale| × |extents|.z / 2 behind
    the viewport plane. The translation must account for the scaled and rotated position
    of |center|. With the identity rotation used for initial fitting, the front face of
    the scaled bounding box lies on the viewport plane and the rest lies behind it.
  1. [=Update the model entity transform=] for the model element, given |transform|.

Note: For initial fitting with an identity rotation, the translation in element space is
(-|scale| × |center|.x, -|scale| × |center|.y, -|scale| × (|center|.z + |extents|.z / 2)). Scaling a model around its own origin
without compensating for its scaled center would not center an off-origin asset.

</div>

### Automatic fitting after resize ### {#automatic-fitting}

Each model element has an <dfn noexport>automatic fitting enabled</dfn> flag, initially true.
Beginning to load a new model resource MUST set this flag to true.

The {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} setter disables [=automatic fitting enabled=]
only after a successful script assignment. An internal update made by a fitting
algorithm MUST NOT disable automatic fitting.

Entering `orbit` mode MUST set [=automatic fitting enabled=] to true and apply the
[=orbit fit algorithm=]. Returning from `orbit` to `none` MUST preserve the current
{{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} and leave automatic fitting enabled; changing modes
back to `none` does not itself refit the model.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="automatic-fitting-after-resize">

When either dimension of the loaded model element's content box changes after layout,
the user agent MUST perform the following steps:

  1. If [=automatic fitting enabled=] is false, preserve
    {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} and return.
  1. If the element is in `orbit` mode, apply the [=orbit fit algorithm=], preserving the
    current rotation.
  1. Otherwise, apply the [=model fit algorithm=] with the current rotation as its <var ignore>rotation</var>
    argument.

</div>

These updates SHOULD be reflected on the next rendered frame. The trigger is a change
to the laid-out content-box dimensions, whether caused by <{model/width}> or
<{model/height}> attributes, CSS sizing, or another layout change. Changing an attribute
without changing those dimensions does not itself trigger a refit.

Note: Given that the {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} is determinative of the model's fit
on the page, resetting to the initial pose can be achieved by storing this initial value
and re-applying it at any point in the future. In `none` mode, re-applying that value
through script also disables automatic fitting until `orbit` mode is entered or a
new model resource begins loading.

### Orbit fit ### {#orbit-fit-section}

The [=orbit fit algorithm=] is used when the model's <{model/stagemode}> enters `orbit`
mode and for automatic fitting while that mode is active.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="orbit-fit">

The <dfn>orbit fit algorithm</dfn> consists of the following steps.

  1. Let |bounds| be the resource's stored bounds, previously calculated using the
    [=bounding box calculation algorithm=].
  1. Let |extents| be the extents of |bounds|.
  1. Let |center| be the center of |bounds|.
  1. Let |diameter| be the Euclidean length of |extents|:
    sqrt(|extents|.x² + |extents|.y² + |extents|.z²). This is the diameter of a sphere
    centered on |center| that encloses the bounding box.
  1. Let |width| and |height| be the laid-out content-box dimensions in CSS pixels, each
    multiplied by `0.0254 / 96` to obtain [=CSS-derived meters=] in [=element space=].
  1. Let |scale| be the minimum of |width| and |height|, divided by |diameter|. If |diameter|
    is zero, set |scale| to 1. If either viewport dimension is zero, set |scale| to 0.
  1. Let |transform| be a matrix preserving the rotation of the model's current
    [=stored entity transform=] and using a uniform scale of |scale|.
  1. Set the translation in |transform| so that |center|, after applying the scale
    and rotation, is aligned with the center of the viewport in X and Y, and is
    |scale| × |diameter| / 2 behind
    the viewport plane. The translation must account for the scaled and rotated position
    of |center|, so that the enclosing sphere lies entirely behind the viewport plane.
  1. [=Update the model entity transform=] for the model element, given |transform|.
  1. Apply subsequent orbit rotations about this fitted center, preserving its position.
    Reflect each resulting matrix by
    [=update the model entity transform|updating the model entity transform=]
    for the model element, given that matrix. The enclosing sphere then remains
    within the viewport at any orientation.

</div>

## Embedded content ## {#embedded-content}

### Replaced-element sizing ### {#model-replaced-element-sizing}

For CSS layout, a <{model}> element MUST be treated as a [=replaced element=]
unless it represents [=fallback content=]. When it represents fallback content,
it MUST be treated as an ordinary element, following
[HTML's embedded-content rendering rules](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#embedded-content-rendering-rules)
for <{object}>.

When replaced, its used width and height follow the CSS sizing rules for
[=replaced elements=] in [[CSS2]] and [[CSS-SIZING-3]], with [=natural dimensions=]
determined below.
Its [=default object size=] is 300 CSS pixels wide and 150 CSS pixels high,
as for <{video}> in [[HTML]]. Object sizing follows [[CSS-IMAGES-3#sizing]].

The <{model/width}> and <{model/height}> attributes MUST map to the corresponding
CSS dimension properties and to ''aspect-ratio'' according to
[HTML's dimension-attribute rendering rules](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/rendering.html#dimRendering)
for <{video}>.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="determine-model-natural-dimensions">

To <dfn noexport>determine model natural dimensions</dfn> for a <{model}> element
|element|:

  1. If |element| represents its [=model/poster frame=], return that image's
    [=natural dimensions=].
  1. Otherwise, return no [=natural dimensions=].

</div>

The user agent MUST use [=determine model natural dimensions=] when sizing the
replaced element. Model geometry, model-space bounds, and
{{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}} MUST NOT supply natural CSS dimensions.

Whether a <{model}> element is [=expose a user interface to the user|exposing a user interface=] is not expected to affect the size
of the rendering; controls are expected to be overlaid above the page content without
causing any layout changes, and may disappear when the user does not need them.

### Poster display ### {#model-poster-display}

For poster display, <dfn noexport>model content is available</dfn> when the selected
model resource is loaded, processed, and ready to display, the condition used to
resolve {{HTMLModelElement/ready}}. Beginning to load a new model resource makes
the previous resource unavailable for the purposes of these display rules.

A <{model}> element MUST represent the content given by the first matching
condition below:

  1. If [=model resource loading is permitted=] is false, one or more model sources
    were specified but source selection has finished without finding a supported
    resource, or the selected model resource cannot be loaded or decoded, the
    element represents its [=fallback content=].
  1. Otherwise, if [=model content is available=], the element represents that
    model content.
  1. Otherwise, if its [=model/poster frame=] is not null, the element represents
    that poster frame.
  1. Otherwise, the element represents transparent black with no natural
    dimensions.

The absence of a model source alone MUST NOT be treated as a load failure for
these display rules. A poster can therefore be displayed before a model source
is provided.

When the element represents its fallback content, it MUST NOT also display its
poster frame. If no fallback content is provided, the element represents
nothing. Beginning a new model load MUST re-evaluate these display rules; a
failure of a previous load MUST NOT prevent the new load's poster from displaying.

Pausing, seeking, looping, or resetting a model's animation MUST NOT, by itself,
cause the poster to be displayed again. A paused model MUST continue to display
its current pose.

When a <{model}> element represents its [=model/poster frame=], the image is
expected to be rendered at the largest size that maintains its aspect ratio
without being taller or wider than the <{model}> element itself, and is expected
to be centered in the <{model}> element. The poster MUST NOT affect the model's
bounding box or {{HTMLModelElement/entityTransform}}.

Note: The poster is a loading preview, whereas fallback content is an alternative
  when the model cannot be displayed. A poster failure does not constitute a model
  failure. Posters are ordinary images; the secure-context restriction on 3D model
  resources and environment maps does not prevent their retrieval. Their requests
  remain subject to [[#cors]], [[#content-security-policy]], and the mixed-content
  checks in [[MIX]].

# Events # {#events}

The {{HTMLModelElement/environmentMapReady}} {{Promise}} resolves when an environment map
resource has been loaded, or is rejected if the resource is unable to be loaded.

The <{model}> element emits a {{HTMLModelElement/ready}} {{Promise}} when the model is
processed and ready to display. The {{Promise}} is rejected if the model source cannot be loaded.

# Formats # {#formats}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-18"></span> Need to investigate what formats are suitable for <{model}>. We might need some kind
  of evaluation matrix. Model can support multiple formats out of the box, but it might be good to
  evaluate what is best of users and developers and why. [GitHub issue #18: Which formats?](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/18).

# HTML Integrations # {#html-integrations}

Additional integrations into HTML:

For the purposes of [[HTML#optional-tags|HTML's optional-tag rules]],
<{model}> is added to the list of parent elements for which a <{p}>
element's end tag MUST NOT be omitted solely because there is no more
content in the parent element.

Note: This matches the rule for <{audio}> and <{video}>. Omission before a
following element remains governed by HTML's existing rules. A <{model}>
start tag does not implicitly close an enclosing <{p}> element.

Note: Because <{model}> is [=flow content=], it is permitted as a child
of <{figure}> by [[HTML#the-figure-element|HTML's existing content model]].
An optional <{figcaption}>, placed as the <{figure}>'s first or last
child, provides a caption for the figure.

<div class="example" id="example-model-figure">

The following example presents a model with fallback content and a caption:

```html
<figure>
  <model src="3d-assets/teapot.glb">
    <img src="image-assets/teapot.png" alt="A ceramic teapot with a curved handle">
  </model>
  <figcaption>A 3D model of a ceramic teapot.</figcaption>
</figure>
```

</div>

## Preload link relationship ## {#preload-link-relationship}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-30"></span> Need to specify Integration with [[HTML#link-type-preload|preload]] link
  relationships. [GitHub issue #30: Integration with `preload`](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/30).

## Integrations with <{source}> element ## {#integrations-with-source-element}

The attributes and mutation hooks for <{source}> children of <{model}>
are defined in [[#source-parent-model]]. Other uses of <{source}> retain
their behavior defined in [[HTML]].

# CSS integrations # {#css-integrations}

## Media Playback States ('':playing'', '':paused'', '':seeking'') ## {#media-playback-states}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-31-css"></span> If [#13](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/13) holds, how does
  the <{model}> work with Media Playback States '':playing'',
  '':paused''? [GitHub issue #31: CSS integrations: Media Playback States](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/31).

## Fullscreen Presentation State: the '':fullscreen'' pseudo-class ## {#fullscreen-presentation-state}

# Fullscreen integration # {#fullscreen-integration}

Fullscreen presentation of <{model}> is governed by [[FULLSCREEN]]. Changes to
its [=content box=] dimensions on entering or leaving fullscreen MUST be handled
according to [[#automatic-fitting]].

# Privacy considerations # {#privacy-considerations}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-36"></span> We need to investigate what the privacy implications are of each model format we will
  recommend. The model formats themselves can fetch resources, so we need to put a privacy and
  security framework around what schemes they can fetch (https only, for instance). We also need to
  say what all the fetch policies are. Need to investigate if the formats provide any guidance here,
  or if they leave it up to the implementation. If they do, we need to specify it (i.e., don't
  send cookies, don't leak the referrer, etc.). [GitHub issue #36: Formats: privacy considerations](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/36).

# Security considerations # {#security-considerations}

## Secure contexts ## {#model-secure-contexts}

For the secure-context checks in this section,
<dfn noexport>model resource loading is permitted</dfn> for a <{model}> element
if and only if its [=node document=]'s
[=relevant settings object=] is a [=secure context=].
The user agent MUST use the [=secure context=] definition in
[[HTML]] to evaluate this condition.

User agents MUST NOT fetch, decode, or render 3D model resources or author-provided
environment maps for a <{model}> element unless [=model resource loading is permitted=]
for that element.

Before starting a model or environment-map load for a <{model}> element, the user
agent MUST check whether [=model resource loading is permitted=] for that element.
If it is not, the user agent MUST NOT start the load and MUST
[=block model resource loading=] for the element.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="model-adopting-steps">

The [=adopting steps=] for a <{model}> element, given |node| and
<var ignore>oldDocument</var>, are:

  1. If [=model resource loading is permitted=] is false for |node|,
    [=block model resource loading=] for |node|.
  1. Otherwise, [=update the model source=] for |node|.

</div>

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="block-model-resource-loading">

To <dfn noexport>block model resource loading</dfn> for a <{model}> element
|element|, the user agent MUST run the following steps:

  1. Abort any pending model or environment-map fetch and decoding operations for
    |element|, and MUST NOT start new ones while loading remains blocked.
  1. Stop rendering |element|'s model and environment map, including any
    previously loaded content.
  1. Set |element|'s [=model/selected source URL=] to null.
  1. Reject any unsettled {{HTMLModelElement/ready}} and
    {{HTMLModelElement/environmentMapReady}} promises of |element|, if they
    exist, with a {{SecurityError}} exception.
  1. Make |element| represent its [=fallback content=] instead of
    the model.

</div>

<div class="note" id="model-secure-context-adoption">

Before an initial load, an insecure context prevents fetching and decoding
from starting; there are no pending operations to abort. The cancellation and
rendering-stop requirements also cover a model that began loading in a secure
document and was subsequently adopted into an insecure document.

Same-origin documents can have different secure-context status. For example,
an HTTPS iframe embedded in an HTTP page is not a secure context because of its
insecure ancestor. That iframe can open a same-origin HTTPS popup, which is a
secure context because it is top-level. If opener access is retained, the popup
can move an already-loading model into the iframe using
`window.opener.document.body.append(model)`. DOM
[=adopt|adoption=] changes the model's node document to the iframe's
document, so loading that was initially permitted must now be stopped.
See [[SECURE-CONTEXTS]] for the distinction between secure contexts and
potentially trustworthy origins.

</div>

The restriction does not prevent fallback descendants from rendering or loading
their own resources under the normal rules for those HTML elements.

The [SecureContext] annotation on {{HTMLModelElement}} restricts exposure of the
interface and its members according to [[WEBIDL]]. It does not replace the
loading and rendering checks above. In particular, a model created in a secure
document MUST NOT continue loading or rendering after being adopted into an
insecure document. These checks MUST also be applied before rendering a model.

Requests for model resources and author-provided environment maps MUST follow
[[FETCH]] and be subject to the mixed-content checks in [[MIX]]. Being in a secure
context does not bypass those checks or other applicable resource-loading policies.
The secure-context requirement itself is not a scheme whitelist for resource URLs.

Note: Secure contexts normally use HTTPS, but the standard also recognizes
  potentially trustworthy local origins. For example,
  `http://localhost`, `http://127.0.0.1`, and
  `http://[::1]` can qualify without a development certificate.
  The usual secure-context rules, including ancestor-context checks, still apply.
  Authors can check {{WindowOrWorkerGlobalScope/isSecureContext}} rather than
  inspecting the URL scheme.

## CORS ## {#cors}

Model resources, author-provided environment maps, and posters MUST be loaded
using [=Fetch=]. Before fetching each request, the user agent MUST
[=configure a model request=] given the request and the element.

<div class="algorithm" data-algorithm="configure-model-request">

To <dfn noexport>configure a model request</dfn>, given a [=/request=] |request|
and a <{model}> element |element|:

  1. Set |request|'s [=request/client=] to |element|'s [=node document=]'s
    [=relevant settings object=].
  1. Set |request|'s [=request/mode=] to `"cors"`.
  1. Set |request|'s [=request/credentials mode=] to the
    [=CORS settings attribute credentials mode=] of |element|'s
    <{model/crossorigin}> attribute.
  1. Set |request|'s [=request/initiator type=] to `"model"`.

</div>

Note: This follows the request setup used by
  [HTML module-script fetching](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#fetch-a-single-module-script)
  and the [font-fetching requirements](https://drafts.csswg.org/css-fonts-4/#font-fetching-requirements)
  in [[CSS-FONTS-4]]. Same-origin `blob:` URLs remain usable, subject to
  [[FETCH]] and the document's CSP.

## Model resource dependencies ## {#formats-and-cors}

As specified in [[#subresource-loading]], model resources MUST be self-contained.
Parsers and renderers MUST NOT retrieve external dependencies; a model requiring
them MUST fail to load. Embedded textures do not cause separate requests and
are covered by the model resource's CSP check.

## Content Security Policy ## {#content-security-policy}

Model resource requests MUST use destination `"model"`, which this specification
adds to the [=destination type=] and {{RequestDestination}} enumeration in
[[FETCH]]. The [=Fetch=] algorithm applies the request client's CSP as defined by
[[CSP3]]. In CSP's [[CSP3#effective-directive-for-a-request|Get the effective directive for request]]
algorithm, `"model"` MUST be handled together with `"audio"`, `"track"`, and
`"video"`, returning [=media-src=]. Environment-map and poster requests MUST use
the existing `"image"` destination and [=img-src=] mapping.

### Fetch failures ### {#model-fetch-failures}

A model or environment-map load that encounters a [=network error=] from Fetch
MUST reject its own unsettled {{HTMLModelElement/ready}} or
{{HTMLModelElement/environmentMapReady}} promise, respectively, with a
{{TypeError}} exception. Model failures follow [[#model-poster-display]];
poster failures follow [=determine a model poster frame=]. A failure MUST affect
only the load that initiated the request.

## Format concerns ## {#format-concerns}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-17"></span> Need to describe that each format will come with its own security considerations (and link to
  the appropriate security considerations in their respective specs). [GitHub issue #17: Format security concerns](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/17).

# Accessibility # {#accessibility}

ISSUE: <span id="issue-50"></span> We need to figure out how to make <{model}> accessible on a number of
  different fronts:
  • Visual: describe what is being presented over time.
  • Interaction:
  describe what can interacted with (regions, buttons, etc.).
  • auditory: describe audio and
  possibly spoke sounds, potentially over time.
  Usually, this would be provide by the
  embedded format... however, it appears that both glTF and USDZ are quite limited when it comes to
  accessibility.
  As such, it may be that we need to leverage what we can from HTML + ARIA to
  overcome the shortcomings of these formats. We have quite a bit of precedent (e.g., from the
  humble, yet limited, `alt` attribute, to how <{canvas}> can be made
  accessibly, to the potential inclusion of <{track}> elements, and so on). [GitHub issue #50: Accessibility of model](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/50).

ISSUE: <span id="issue-39"></span> We need to define how what the ARIA semantics are and what is exposed (application probably).
  We need to coordinate with the accessibility folks + get this added to the HTML Accessibility API
  Mappings. [GitHub issue #39: Accessibility: ARIA integration and HTML Accessibility API Mappings](https://github.com/immersive-web/model-element/issues/39).

